when it relied on the Parental Alienation Syndrome as a basis for rejecting her
[the mother’s] claims that [the father] had abused the child.</q> While the appellate
court did not rule on the merits of PAS, it did caution the family court that if
PAS <q>is raised in the rehearing, there should be evidence to support its validity.</q>
